## Why This Question Is Asked
This problem evaluates the ability to extend classic DP patterns (like the staircase problem) with an additional resource dimension, while also testing space optimization skills and recursive thinking via memoization. It distinguishes candidates who understand both bottom-up and top-down approaches, and who reason through edge cases systematically.

## Key Concepts
- DP with an extra budget constraint (two-dimensional state: position + accumulated cost)
- Space optimization using rolling arrays to reduce O(n·B) space to O(B)
- Memoization on composite state (i, b) for the recursive variant
- Correct base case handling when starting from a virtual position before index 0

## Code Example
```javascript
const MOD = 1_000_000_007n;

function countWays(cost, B) {
  const n = cost.length;
  if (n === 0) return 0;

  // bottom-up with rolling arrays
  let prev2 = new Array(B + 1).fill(0);
  let prev1 = new Array(B + 1).fill(0);

  // base: step 0
  if (cost[0] <= B) prev2[cost[0]] = 1;

  // base: step 1
  if (n > 1 && cost[1] <= B) prev1[cost[1]] = 1;

  // step 2 onwards
  for (let i = 2; i < n; i++) {
    const cur = new Array(B + 1).fill(0);
    for (let b = cost[i]; b <= B; b++) {
      cur[b] = (prev1[b - cost[i]] + prev2[b - cost[i]]) % MOD;
    }
    prev2 = prev1;
    prev1 = cur;
  }

  return n === 1 ? prev2[B] : prev1[B];
}

// memoized DFS variant
function countWaysDFS(cost, B) {
  const n = cost.length;
  const memo = new Map();

  function dfs(i, b) {
    if (b < 0) return 0;
    if (i < 0) return b === 0 ? 1 : 0;
    const key = `${i},${b}`;
    if (memo.has(key)) return memo.get(key);
    const result = (dfs(i - 1, b - cost[i]) + dfs(i - 2, b - cost[i])) % MOD;
    memo.set(key, result);
    return result;
  }

  return dfs(n - 1, B);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests dynamic dictionary updates with a prefix-based query, combining trie augmentation and edge-case handling. 

## Key Concepts
- Trie with per-node passCount to track subtree content
- O(length of word) updates for insert/delete
- LCP query via prefix traversal until no valid child
- Edge cases: duplicates, deleting absent words, memory pruning

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.next = new Map();
    this.cnt = 0;
  }
}
class DynamicDict {
  constructor(){ this.root = new TrieNode(); this.hasWord=new Set(); }
  insert(w){ if(this.hasWord.has(w)) return; this.hasWord.add(w); let node=this.root; for(const ch of w){ if(!node.next.has(ch)) node.next.set(ch,new TrieNode()); node=node.next.get(ch); node.cnt++; } }
  delete(w){ if(!this.hasWord.has(w)) return; this.hasWord.delete(w); let node=this.root; for(const ch of w){ const child=node.next.get(ch); child.cnt--; if(child.cnt===0){ node.next.delete(ch); } node=child; } }
  lcpQuery(q){ let node=this.root, depth=0, res=0; for(const ch of q){ if(!node.next.has(ch)) break; node=node.next.get(ch); depth++; if(node.cnt>0) res=depth; } return res; }
}
```

